1
00:00:02.002 --> 00:00:04.295
Welcome to MCP: Build

2
00:00:04.295 --> 00:00:08.299
Rich-Context AI Apps with Anthropic built
in partnership with Anthropic.

3
00:00:08.508 --> 00:00:11.553
In this course,
you'll learn the core concepts of MCP

4
00:00:11.553 --> 00:00:14.514
and how to implement it in your
AI application.

5
00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:19.185
The Model Context Protocol,
or MCP, is an open protocol

6
00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:23.440
that standardizes how your LLM applications
can get access to context

7
00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:26.693
in terms of tools and data resources

8
00:00:26.735 --> 00:00:29.404
based on the client-server architecture.

9
00:00:29.404 --> 00:00:33.199
It defines how communication takes place
between an MCP client

10
00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:36.536
hosted inside your own LLM application,
and an MCP server

11
00:00:36.745 --> 00:00:40.915
that exposes tools and data resources
and prompt templates to your application.

12
00:00:41.666 --> 00:00:45.211
Since Anthropic
launched MCP in November of 2024,

13
00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.423
the MCP ecosystem has been growing
really rapidly.

14
00:00:48.673 --> 00:00:52.302
I'm delighted that the instructor for this
course is Elie Schoppik,

15
00:00:52.635 --> 00:00:55.638
who is Head of Technical Education
at Anthropic.

16
00:00:55.764 --> 00:00:58.224
Thanks, Andrew.
I'm excited to teach this course with you.

17
00:00:58.224 --> 00:01:01.227
MCP originated
as part of an internal project

18
00:01:01.269 --> 00:01:05.356
where we recognized an opportunity to
extend the capabilities of Claude Desktop

19
00:01:05.607 --> 00:01:09.569
so that it can interact with local file
systems and other external systems.

20
00:01:10.028 --> 00:01:13.531
We found the protocol we developed
was useful in many AI applications,

21
00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:17.160
with similar needs. To make this available
to more developers,

22
00:01:17.368 --> 00:01:18.953
we published the specification

23
00:01:18.953 --> 00:01:21.414
and opened its development
to the open source community.

24
00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:25.752
The MCP ecosystem includes
a growing number of MCP service

25
00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:30.340
developed by the open source community,
as well as by Anthropic's MCP team.

26
00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:34.344
MCP is model agnostic
and is designed to be easy

27
00:01:34.344 --> 00:01:36.638
to plug into multiple applications.

28
00:01:36.638 --> 00:01:39.182
Say you're building a research
assistant agent,

29
00:01:39.182 --> 00:01:42.977
and you'd like for this agent to interact
with your GitHub repos, read notes

30
00:01:42.977 --> 00:01:46.106
from your Google Drive documents,
maybe create a summary stored

31
00:01:46.106 --> 00:01:50.193
in your local system. Instead of you
writing your own custom LLM tools,

32
00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:55.073
you can connect your agent to the GitHub,
Google Drive and File System service,

33
00:01:55.406 --> 00:01:58.326
which will provide the tool or the API call

34
00:01:58.326 --> 00:02:01.913
definitions
and also handle the tool execution.

35
00:02:02.038 --> 00:02:05.291
Elie will walk you
through the details of the MCP protocol.

36
00:02:05.834 --> 00:02:09.921
We'll first dive into the details
of the MCP client-server architecture.

37
00:02:10.004 --> 00:02:13.675
You'll then work on a chatbot
application to make it MCP compatible.

38
00:02:14.134 --> 00:02:17.720
You'll build and test an MCP server
and connect your chatbot to it.

39
00:02:18.263 --> 00:02:18.930
Your MCP

40
00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:23.059
server will provide tools, prompt
templates, and resources to your chatbot.

41
00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:26.896
You'll also connect your chatbot
to other trusted third-party servers

42
00:02:27.063 --> 00:02:29.107
to extend its capabilities.

43
00:02:29.107 --> 00:02:31.901
You'll then re-use your MCP server
and connect it

44
00:02:31.901 --> 00:02:34.904
to other MCP applications
like Claude Desktop.

45
00:02:35.155 --> 00:02:38.741
Finally, you'll learn how you can deploy
your MCP server remotely.

46
00:02:39.117 --> 00:02:41.828
I'd like to thank from DeepLearning.AI,

47
00:02:41.828 --> 00:02:44.831
Hawraa Salami,
who had contributed to this course.

48
00:02:45.081 --> 00:02:47.458
MCP is a really important technology

49
00:02:47.458 --> 00:02:50.670
that's making it much easier for LLM application
developers

50
00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:53.673
to connect the systems
to many tools and data resources.

51
00:02:54.257 --> 00:02:57.135
And for teams
building tools or providing data,

52
00:02:57.135 --> 00:02:59.012
it is also making it much easier

53
00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:02.223
to make what they build
available to many developers.

54
00:03:02.891 --> 00:03:05.727
So this is a technology
worth learning about.

55
00:03:05.727 --> 00:03:09.272
The next video goes through
why connecting LLM applications

56
00:03:09.314 --> 00:03:14.314
to resources had been so difficult before,
and how MCP addresses this.

57
00:03:14.694 --> 00:03:17.280
So, please
go on to the next video to learn more.

